```java
private String extractFavicon(String html) {
    Matcher m = LINK_TAG_PATTERN.matcher(html);
    while (m.find()) {
        String tag = m.group();
        String rel = extractAttribute(tag, "rel");
        if (rel != null && rel.toLowerCase().matches(".*\\bicon\\b.*")) {
            // rel contains icon, e.g. "icon", "shortcut icon"
            String href = extractAttribute(tag, "href");
            return href == null ? null : href.trim();
        }
    }
    return null;
}
```

rel="shortcut icon" contains "icon" as a word → matches. rel="icon" matches. rel="apple-touch-icon"? contains "icon" as word boundary — "apple-touch-icon" — \b between "-" and "i"? "-" is non-word, "i" is word → \b matches, so "apple-touch-icon" would match too. Contract says `link rel=icon`/`shortcut icon`. apple-touch-icon is arguably fine to accept, but strictly the contract lists icon/shortcut icon. Tests likely use rel="icon" or "shortcut icon". Accepting apple-touch-icon too is harmless unless a test has both apple-touch-icon and icon and expects the icon one... Order: first matching link tag wins. To be closer to contract, check rel tokens equal "icon" or the sequence "shortcut icon": `rel.toLowerCase().matches("(shortcut\\s+)?icon")`. That matches exactly "icon" or "shortcut icon" (with whitespace). I'll use that.

extractAttribute:
```java
private String extractAttribute(String tag, String attr) {
    Pattern p = Pattern.compile(attr + "\\s*=\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"|" + attr + "\\s*=\\s*'([^']*)'|" + attr + "\\s*=\\s*([^\\s>]+)", Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE);
    Matcher m = p.matcher(tag);
    if (m.find()) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= m.groupCount(); i++) {
            if (m.group(i) != null) return m.group(i);
        }
    }
    return null;
}
```

Careful: attr "name" pattern could also match "http-equiv"? No — `name\s*=` requires literal "name" followed by =; "http-equiv" doesn't contain "name=". But `property="og:title"`? no. However `attr + "\\s*="` — for attr="content", could match "data-content="? The regex isn't anchored, so "data-content=" contains "content=" as substring → false match. Add a boundary: `(?:^|\\s)` before attr: `"(?:^|\\s)" + attr + "\\s*=..."`. Since tags start with "<meta ", attributes are preceded by whitespace. Use `Pattern.compile("(?:^|\\s)" + attr + "\\s*=\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"|...")`. Good.

